    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d359">

                <pb facs="0314" n="308" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg308"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.115/598: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-03-30T20:00:00+09:00">March 30, 1939—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 31—6 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>156. Department’s 75, March 27, 7 p.m.<anchor n="37" xml:id="d359-f1"/> Our note
                    modified in accordance with the Department’s suggestions was presented to the
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs orally today.<anchor n="38" xml:id="d359-f2"/>
                    After reading to the Minister the principal points brought out in the note, I
                    said to him that this situation is becoming increasingly serious and that these
                    continual bombings of American property, already totaling 135 in number, reports
                    of which are coming across my desk almost daily, are causing increasing
                    perturbation in the United States. I pointed <pb facs="0315" n="309"
                        xml:id="pg309"/> out especially a few of the outstanding cases including
                    that of the American Lutheran Mission at Tungpeh and that of the American
                    Southern Baptist Hospital at Chengchow which had been bombed seven times since
                    February 1938 and then urged with all earnestness that effective steps be taken
                    to put an end to these bombings, stating that the American Government will
                    carefully watch future developments in the expectation that the Japanese
                    Government by systematic effective measures will relieve the American Government
                    of the obligation to publish the note.</p>
                <p>The Minister expressed deep regret at the report which I had laid before him and
                    said that he would immediately take the matter up with the appropriate
                        authorities.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d359-f3"/></p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
